  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Herpes is a common, lifelong sexually transmitted infection (STI) caused by the herpes simplex virus (HSV). It is categorized into two main types: HSV-1 (commonly oral, but increasingly genital) and HSV-2 (commonly genital), both of which cause painful blisters or ulcers. It spreads through skin-to-skin contact during vaginal, anal, or oral sex, even without symptoms. Key Details About Herpes: Types: HSV-1 (usually cold sores) and HSV-2 (usually genital herpes). Transmission: Highly contagious, transmitted via skin-to-skin contact, saliva, or fluid from lesions. Symptoms: Many people have no symptoms, but it can cause painful genital/oral blisters, itching, and sores. Treatment: While there is no cure, antiviral medicines can manage symptoms and reduce the risk of transmission. Management: It is a chronic infection that can have recurring outbreaks, which often become less frequent over time.",
